REMAINS before option-keyword.sexp: the loader's format is (international .
local) pairs, and both runners are FRENCH installs, so the international
keyword of each prompt is not measured. Pair them by position against an
English install's prompts (or the vendors' English documentation for these
13 commands, cited), and extract the abbreviation letters (the capitals:
"aNnuler" -> N). Note that the two products' keyword sets differ (OFFSET:
AutoCAD [Par/Effacer/Calque], BricsCAD [Passer par le point/Effacer/Calque]),
so the dictionary is per product.


## Pairing with the international keywords (2026-10-07, clautolisp 2.2.229)

The harvested local keywords are now PAIRED with their international keywords,
per product, and shipped: clautolisp/cadtui/data/locale/fr_FR/option-keyword.sexp
(57 pairs), loaded by cadtui like the other locale files.

How
- The two first-cut harvests are committed (re-generated from the job
  artifacts 16981155886 / 16981155888 with the converter):
  probe-results/optkw/fr_FR/autocad-2022-windows.sexp and
  bricscad-v25-windows.sexp. The converter now splits an option list only on
  the slashes OUTSIDE parentheses, so BricsCAD ZOOM's "Échelle (nx/nxp)" is
  one keyword (it was cut in two in the 2026-10-06 list above).
- probe-results/optkw/fr_FR/international-en.json is the pairing table: per
  product, per command, the English reference URL, the harvested local list
  (the converter REFUSES the pairing if the harvest no longer matches it) and
  the aligned international list (null = not paired).
- `scripts/cadtui-optkw-to-sexp.py --pair probe-results/optkw/fr_FR/international-en.json
  -o clautolisp/cadtui/data/locale/fr_FR/option-keyword.sexp` writes the
  dictionary: ((:AUTOCAD ("_CMD" (international . local) ...) ...) (:BRICSCAD ...)).
  The URL of each command is written above it in the data file.
- Pairing rule: position AND meaning against the vendor's English command
  reference; every settled pair below agrees on both (same count, same order,
  same meaning). The international string is the documented option label
  (BricsCAD's reference shows the prompt capitals, e.g. mEthod; Autodesk's
  pages show plain headings, e.g. Method) -- not a measured English prompt, so
  the English abbreviation letters are NOT claimed.
- The local abbreviation is not stored: OPTION-KEYWORD-ABBREVIATION reads it
  off the local keyword (its capitals: aNnuler -> N, ANgle -> AN; or its
  parenthesised capitals: "étendu (ET)" -> ET).

cadtui API (exported from clautolisp.cadtui):
  (local-option-keyword "_OFFSET" "Through" "fr_FR" :product :autocad) => "Par"
  (international-option-keyword "_CHAMFER" "N" "fr_FR" :product :autocad) => "Undo"
  (international-option-keyword "_OFFSET" "P" "fr_FR" :product :bricscad-v25) => "Through point"
  A whole local keyword or its abbreviation resolves; "_kw" forces the
  international form; a dialect name folds onto its product; anything unknown
  passes through (identity fallback). Tests: 5 FiveAM tests (45 checks) in
  cadtui/tests/locale-tests.lisp.

Sources
